      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Melt the butter in a wide frying pan over medium heat. Add the onion and cook, stirring, for 8 to 10 minutes until softened and lightly golden."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the drained sauerkraut, caraway seeds, and bay leaves. Pour in the white wine. Stir to combine, reduce the heat to low, cover, and braise for 20 minutes, stirring occasionally."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "While the sauerkraut braises, heat a griddle pan or second frying pan over medium-high heat with the oil. Add the bratwurst and cook for 12 to 15 minutes, turning every few minutes, until well browned on all sides and cooked through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste the sauerkraut and adjust with a pinch of sugar if it is very sharp. Remove the bay leaves."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve the bratwurst on a bed of sauerkraut with wholegrain mustard alongside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sauerkraut is too sour"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rinse it under cold water before cooking and drain thoroughly. A small pinch of sugar in the braising step also helps balance the acidity."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Bratwurst splits during cooking"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The heat is too high. Cook over a steady medium to medium-high flame. Do not score the skins."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sauerkraut is dry"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add a splash of water or extra wine and put the lid back on for 5 more minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bratwurst and sauerkraut is the plate you find at every German outdoor festival, market stall, and beer garden. It is food built around contrast: the rich, salty sausage against the sharp, fermented cabbage. Bavaria claims it most loudly, but the whole country eats it. Pretzels and a cold lager are the natural companions."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
